<!-- Phase 9 website teaching layer; original chapter remains read-only. Evidence: website/reviews/android-phase9-findings.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">The team proposes sharing half the app, but nobody can name which duplicated defect or delivery delay this will solve. Begin with one measurable problem and one reversible seam. A pilot earns expansion by improving that outcome while both platform teams can still ship and debug.</p>

<!-- @after 1. When NOT to Use KMP :: visual -->
### A pilot has three owners

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="2"><div class="node"><strong>Product outcome</strong><small>The defect, consistency or lead-time change to measure.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Shared contract</strong><small>Inputs, results, errors, data and lifecycle.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Platform consumption</strong><small>Android and iOS integration, debugging and release.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Stop condition</strong><small>Evidence for expanding, repairing, holding or rolling back.</small></div></div><figcaption>Reuse percentage alone does not establish value. Include the new tooling and ownership cost in the comparison.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 2. The Incremental Migration Path (Strangler Fig) :: animation -->
### Replace one dependency through a seam

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A pilot remains reversible until it earns expansion</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="contract" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Existing product contract</strong><small data-text="contractV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="old" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Current implementation</strong><small data-text="oldV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="shared" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Shared candidate</strong><small data-text="sharedV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="evidence" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Pilot evidence</strong><small data-text="evidenceV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Freeze behaviour",
"caption": "Capture the current public contract and baseline before replacing its implementation.",
"state": {
"contract": "active",
"old": "done"
},
"text": {
"contractV": "behaviour and data contracts",
"oldV": "serves production",
"sharedV": "not selected",
"evidenceV": "baseline recorded"
},
"add": [
"contract",
"old",
"shared",
"evidence"
]
},
{
"label": "Build beside it",
"caption": "Introduce a candidate behind the seam and exercise both through appropriate contract tests.",
"state": {
"shared": "active"
},
"text": {
"sharedV": "candidate implementation"
}
},
{
"label": "Select safely",
"caption": "Switch a bounded path or cohort with a deliberate rollback mechanism. Avoid unowned dual writes.",
"state": {
"evidence": "active"
},
"text": {
"evidenceV": "correctness + performance + team cost"
}
},
{
"label": "Decide from results",
"caption": "Expand, repair or reverse against the agreed thresholds; do not equate moved files with success.",
"state": {
"evidence": "done"
},
"text": {
"evidenceV": "explicit continue / hold / reverse decision"
}
},
{
"label": "Retire after proof",
"caption": "Remove the old path only when both platform consumers and release operations meet the exit criteria.",
"state": {
"old": "ghost",
"shared": "done"
},
"text": {
"oldV": "retired after exit criteria"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>The dependency graph and product risk determine the next slice. The chapter’s phase order is a heuristic, not a required rewrite sequence.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 5. Organizational Buy-In Strategies :: check -->
### Try the decision

<details><summary>Only a quarter of code is shared. Failed migration?</summary>

That number is insufficient. Inspect whether the selected high-value boundaries improve the agreed outcomes after build, release and maintenance costs.
</details>

<details><summary>iOS cannot reproduce a framework build. Expand the pilot?</summary>

Repair ownership and reproducible integration first. An unowned dependency makes production adoption harder to support.
</details>

<details><summary>Two implementations write the same store during rollout. Safe by default?</summary>

No. Define consistency, ownership and rollback before dual writes. Prefer reversible selection and contract comparisons where they can establish the required behaviour.
</details>
